id: bible-in-a-year-five-days
title: The Bible in a year, five days a week
version: 1
creator: Day by Word
summary: The 66 books of the Protestant canon, Genesis to Revelation, in
  the order they are printed, in 260 readings of whole chapters balanced by
  reading time: five readings a week for 52 weeks, with two rest days in
  each week.
suits: Readers who want to read every book in a year without reading every
  day, and can give about a quarter of an hour on five days a week, and up
  to twenty minutes for the longest readings.
schedule: sequence
behaviors: pace, finish-date
reading-days: 5
coverage: whole-bible
canon: protestant
books: all
limitations: It reads the 66 books only; the deuterocanonical books (also
  called the Apocrypha), which Catholic and Orthodox Bibles include, are
  not in it. It reads in printed order, not the order of events, and the
  New Testament begins at reading 200. With fewer reading days, its
  readings are longer than those of the Bible in a year, which reads every
  day.
instructions: Read the next reading on each of your five reading days, in
  any Bible; the other two days of the week are rest days. If you miss a
  day, your next reading waits for you. To finish by a date, choose a
  finish date and Day by Word will show what each reading day asks.
source: An original plan by Day by Word. The Old Testament and the New are
  each divided into readings of whole chapters, as even in reading time as
  whole chapters allow; some readings run from the end of one book into the
  next. Reading times are estimated from word counts of the World English
  Bible, a public-domain translation; the plan can be read in any
  translation.
rights: daybyword-original
review: reviewed
